{"question": "A 36-year-old woman, gravida 2, para 2, comes to the emergency department because of sudden-onset, severe right flank pain. She was in her aerobics class when the pain started but denies any trauma to the region. She has a history of recurrent ovarian cysts. Menses occur regularly at 28-day intervals. Her temperature is 37.1\u00b0C (99.3\u00b0F). Abdominal examination shows tenderness in the right lower quadrant with guarding. Pelvic ultrasound shows a large simple cyst on the right ovary. Right ovarian artery flow is detectable on Doppler, but there is no flow detected in the right ovarian vein. Which of the following ligaments is most likely to have been involved?", "options": {"A": "Broad ligament", "B": "Round ligament of the uterus", "C": "Ovarian ligament", "D": "Cardinal ligament", "E": "Infundibulopelvic ligament"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "E", "question_domains": ["Obstetrics and Gynecology", "Emergency Medicine", "Radiology", "Ultrasound", "Vascular Surgery"], "option_domains": ["Gynecology", "Obstetrics"], "question_analyses": {"Obstetrics and Gynecology": "The scenario described involves a 36-year-old woman with a history of recurrent ovarian cysts who presents to the emergency department with sudden-onset, severe right flank pain. The patient's medical history and current symptoms suggest a potential ovarian cyst-related complication, specifically torsion of the right ovary. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n1. **Clinical Presentation:**\n   - **Sudden-onset severe pain:** This is a hallmark symptom of ovarian torsion. - **Right flank pain:** Pain localized to the right flank can be indicative of ovarian torsion, especially in the context of a known ovarian cyst. - **Tenderness in the right lower quadrant with guarding:** This finding on physical examination is consistent with ovarian torsion, which can cause significant inflammation and pain. - **Regular menstrual cycle:** This suggests that the pain is not related to menst